  - 4 analysis lines (baseline reproduction, slippage and planned-risk recomputation, bootstrap, truncation).
  This is within the declared budget.
- Author's trials: 12 cells on this sleeve, 120 in the lens.
- Hard rules kept:
  - nothing dated 2022 or later was read or simulated (frames and trade files filtered while being read);
  - no file of the lab was modified, and no git command was run;
  - NUMBA_CACHE_DIR was under /tmp and PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE was set;
  - I read the author's code only after my own numbers were fixed.
- Cause of the 3-trade gap with the author: their chain tests the one-position rule at the rule's touch bar (common.py chain() on S.act = the rule's entry_i), while the engine's no_overlap would test at the limit's activation bar. The specification must state which one a live robot uses.
- A pointer, not attacked here: the author's market-fallback variants on this sleeve keep the runaway breakouts. RT30_mkt|price (+36.36 R in 2018-2021, +212.36 R before 2018) and RT120_mkt|price (+35.64 / +207.84) improve both periods against the rule's +27.06 / +204.09; I reproduced their numbers exactly. A plain market entry one bar after the touch also gives +30.18 / +204.44 R.
- Any gain of this kind depends on how much a gold buy-stop really slips at FP Markets during the London morning; that should be measured on the demo account.
